{
  "$schema": "../signalset.schema.json",
  "vehicle": "Cadillac-Lyriq-2025",
  "description": "2025 Cadillac Lyriq (GM Ultium) diagnostics. 29-bit CAN (ISO 15765-4, ATSP7; 11-bit is gateway-blocked). Confirmed on-vehicle: pack CURRENT (ECU 17), module voltages, battery temperatures, odometer, EVSE pilot. SOC / live pack voltage / per-cell voltages / capacity registers are on gateway-blocked ECUs (CB and the cell monitor) and are NOT reachable via the OBD port; capacity is instead measured by coulomb-counting a charge between target cutoffs (measured 292 Ah / ~103 kWh = ~100% SOH on 2026-08-15). See docs/lyriq-did-map.md and docs/lyriq-re-log.md.",
  "provenance": {
    "derivedFrom": "Live 2025 Lyriq (VIN 1GYKPTRL5SZ302606) via a Pi BLE bridge + Viecar ELM327 clone, cross-referenced with the community OBDb database and the Chevy Bolt PID set (partial Ultium crossover: 2414 current family + 434F temp carry over; 41A3 capacity / 8334 & 43AF SOC do NOT — rejected in every vehicle mode). See docs/lyriq-re-log.md.",
    "confidence": "CONFIRMED on-vehicle: protocol, ECU map, pack current (17.2414 s16/20 A, sign checked both directions), nominal pack V (17.2429 /64), module voltages (40.416C-E /100), temps (40.4127/4124/40E5/40E6 /32, 40.434F -40), odometer (40.448F /64 km vs dash), EVSE pilot (40.4149 /10 A vs charger), 12V rail (33E5 /10, multi-ECU). NOT available via port: SOC, live pack V, per-cell V, GM capacity register (gateway-blocked; DoIP candidate). Register availability is wake-mode-dependent: unattended charging = sparse ECU 40.",
    "license": "Battery signals cross-referenced with OBDb/Cadillac-LYRIQ (CC BY-SA). J1979 signals are public-domain."
  },
  "protocol": {
    "canFormat": "29bit",
    "obdProtocol": 7,
    "note": "CONFIRMED on-vehicle: ISO 15765-4 CAN 29-bit/500k, force with ATSP7 (auto-detect ATSP0 FAILS on the Lyriq). Battery/propulsion controller (BECM) is ECU 40: request header 18DA40F1, response filter 18DAF140. A Viecar ELM327 clone DID handle 29-bit multiframe here, though an STN adapter (OBDLink CX/MX+) is still recommended for reliability."
  },
